      "writtenOn": "2025-02-27T00:49:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Does this work for AOT-compiled code? Or do we need to pass a flag to dex2oat (in dexpreopt, odrefresh, and artd) to make it emit the check? Could you please verify it locally?",
      "revId": "770f0dd9fc7a59cab0c94b36871e732341665742",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "87480900_bb744e42",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1373864
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-02-27T06:58:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Good questions. Hiddenapi checks are done at link time when compiling. Since this is a strengthening of them, a gap there will result in an access slipping by as allowed until the oat file is invalidated for other reasons. Hence the failure mode is benign - the worst effect is that an error may get somewhat more confusing if it\u0027s delayed.\n\nThe goal here is to target the case of a preloaded module in new Baklava system images, and then this isn\u0027t a factor for on-device dex2oat invocations. Build time checks on host (covering dexpreopt artifacts, if dexpreopting is enabled) are a different matter, and the plan is to address them by running veridex.\n\nThe remaining case is odrefresh after OTA. That\u0027s also beside the goal, but in any case it\u0027s run after reboot so it\u0027s no different from any other invocation in this regard.\n\n\u003e Could you please verify it locally?\n\nYes, updated the test description.",
      "parentUuid": "c1ed3da4_c2d59166",
      "revId": "770f0dd9fc7a59cab0c94b36871e732341665742",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8936d357_ca4a5109",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1884045
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-02-27T07:21:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for verifying the dex2oat started from odrefresh. I realized that dex2oat and the runtime shares the same code around `core_platform_api_policy_` in runtime.cc, so my concern doesn\u0027t really exist.\n\nSo since dex2oat also has hidden api enforcement enabled, I assume it will emit code that triggers a runtime exception at runtime on hidden api access, so there won\u0027t be any access slipping by, right? Otherwise, I think this will need more discussion, because for framework jars and system server jars, the oat files are never supposed to be invalid, because odrefresh refreshes their oat files whenever they get outdated.\n\n\u003e Build time checks on host (covering dexpreopt artifacts, if dexpreopting is enabled) are a different matter, and the plan is to address them by running veridex.\n\nInteresting. That\u0027s the first time I heard of this tool. Does that run on build host? If that works, why do we still need the runtime check? Are you worried about the build-time check can be easily bypassed by OEMs?",
      "parentUuid": "87480900_bb744e42",
      "revId": "770f0dd9fc7a59cab0c94b36871e732341665742",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5e2e7f79_eccef938",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1373864
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-02-27T18:19:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "My understanding is that dex2oat will just resort to runtime resolution if it cannot resolve the API at link time, i.e. only delaying the failure.\n\nWe really shouldn\u0027t have accesses to the now blocked APIs in the BCP or anywhere else in our codebase, and my earlier CLs in the chain were to handle some cases of that. So in that sense it\u0027s still supposed to never be invalid. Are you talking about odrefresh recompiling the BCP over and over at every boot if that still was to happen?\n\n\u003e That\u0027s the first time I heard of this tool. Does that run on build host?\n\nIndeed I also became aware of veridex just yesterday. It is a [host binary](https://cs.android.com/android/platform/superproject/main/+/main:art/tools/veridex/Android.bp;l\u003d24-27;drc\u003dd30b340648378d269920519f469ca197e47dcb27) with the main purpose of performing static hidden API checks at build time.\n\n\u003e If that works, why do we still need the runtime check? Are you worried about the build-time check can be easily bypassed by OEMs?\n\nStatic checks are never 100% - there can e.g. be reflection (although I\u0027m told that veridex makes some effort to detect trivial cases of that too). I see them as no more than a complement to runtime checks.",
      "parentUuid": "8936d357_ca4a5109",
      "revId": "770f0dd9fc7a59cab0c94b36871e732341665742",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
